rpresser@gmail.com says...
> Perhaps an revised version of the hearse client could read the tail of
> the logfile, comparing the bonesfile creation date to the death date in
> the logfile, to retrieve the cause of death in other cases.  Ambitious
> and somewhat invasive, I realize, and hackable too...

Yeah, unfortunately the timestamp in the logfile isn't granular enough - 
only the date is stored and not the time.  Also the way in which the 
logfile refers to the level number isn't always easily convertible to a 
bones filename.  Those two together mean that there's no easy way to 
know whether your death on L5 of the Gnomish Mines corresponds to 
BONM0.5 or not - maybe you entered the level, didn't load the bones file 
you created earlier that day, and then went on to die.

I did submit a request to the Dev Team to include a logfile timestamp 
with the date, but I'm not holding my breath for it.

There are other problems with the causes of death stored on Hearse, for 
example, it can be fooled by multiple graves on a single level or by 
non-grave inscriptions.  Using the logfile would definitely be better, I 
agree.

